text:
maria: Quick sync — I'm trying to finalize the JS SDK's fetch/fallback strategy. Goal: one package that works in ESM + CJS, Node (18+), Bun, Deno, and edge runtimes without forcing app authors to polyfill. Any take on undici vs global fetch + typings impact?

sam: +1 on single package. My recommendation: runtime opt-in + sensible automatic fallbacks. SDK accepts an explicit `fetch` option first, then uses globalThis.fetch if present, then tries to require/import undici (only in Node), and finally falls back to a tiny runtime polyfill if user wants it.

jonathan: That aligns with what we've discussed. Important: don't bundle undici by default — dynamic import to avoid breaking Deno/Edge. Also ship CJS and ESM builds via conditional exports so CJS consumers still work.

lisa: Minor nit on types: if we require consumers to have `lib.dom` for fetch types, it forces DOM types into node projects which can clash. Instead export a small Fetch signature type and let consumers pass any compatible function. Example: `type FetchFn = (input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>` — keep SDK types agnostic.

maria: Love the typed callback approach. Can we sketch packaging and runtime detection snippets here so I can drop them into the PR?

sam: Sure. Packaging (package.json) suggestion:
```json
{
  "name": "@redwood/sdk",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
      "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js"
    },
    "./package.json": "./package.json"
  },
  "type": "module",
  "types": "./dist/types/index.d.ts"
}
```
This keeps ESM as default but preserves a CJS entry for consumers using `require`.

jonathan: For runtime fetch resolution (pseudocode):
```js
async function resolveFetch(optionFetch) {
  if (optionFetch) return optionFetch;
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch; // edge, bun, modern node
  // Try undici for node consumers
  try {
    // dynamic import so bundlers/Deno/Edge don't pull undici
    const undici = await import('undici');
    return undici.fetch || (undici.default && undici.default.fetch);
  } catch (e) {
    // no undici available
  }
  // last resort: runtime polyfill we ship as a separate small module
  return (await import('./runtime-polyfills/node-fetch-lite.js')).fetch;
}
```
This avoids requiring undici for edge/deno builds and keeps the main bundle small.

lisa: For CommonJS builds we should do a try/require fallback too, because dynamic import might be awkward inside CJS: `let undici; try { undici = require('undici'); } catch {}` — but hide that behind a util so consumers don't see it.

maria: Good call. What about recessed typings so TS projects don't need dom lib?

sam: Provide an opt-in type that doesn't pull in lib.dom. Example in `dist/types/index.d.ts`:
```ts
export type FetchLike = (input: any, init?: any) => Promise<any>;
export interface SDKOptions {
  fetch?: FetchLike;
  // other options
}
```
Keep the types intentionally permissive; then provide a separate `types-dom.d.ts` file that re-exports the stricter dom-based types and document it for users who want perfect typings.

jonathan: Also document the recommended pattern in README: encourage passing the runtime fetch for deterministic behavior in serverless or unusual runtimes. Example usage: `new RedwoodClient({ fetch: myCustomFetch })`.

maria: CI note: ci-bot, can we add a smoke test matrix that runs quick bundling/tests in Node, Bun, and edge (node18 with --experimental-fetch off) to catch regressions?

ci-bot: Added pipeline job `sdk/fetch-compat-smoke` — runs Node 18/20, Bun latest, and a lightweight edge worker run. Fails if package import triggers undici resolution in non-node runtimes.

lisa: One more gotcha: bundlers like webpack/rollup can inline `globalThis.fetch` detection incorrectly if code is string-replace optimized. Keep detection in a tiny helper file (not inlined) so build tools can't tree-shake our runtime checks.

maria: Excellent. I'll implement: 1) `fetch` option into client ctor, 2) dynamic undici import only when needed, 3) permissive internal types + optional dom types file, 4) package.json exports for cjs/esm, 5) docs snippet and smoke tests.

sam: If you want I can draft the PR with the helper util + package.json diffs.

maria: Please do. Thanks y'all, will tag reviewers once PR is ready. :pray:

jonathan: FYI - also mention Deno/Bun in README so community doesn't assume undici will work there.

lisa: And add a note about source maps for CJS build — it helps debugging when people require our cjs entry.

maria: Awesome. I'll follow up with the PR link.

ci-bot: PR template reminder: include `smoke:fetch-compat` label to trigger the new matrix.
